== Incumbents defeated ==

=== In primary elections ===
# {{ushr|Louisiana|5|X}}: [[Vance McAllister]] (R) lost a [[Nonpartisan blanket primary]] to Jamie Mayo (D) and [[Ralph Abraham (politician)|Ralph Abraham]] (R). Abraham then won the runoff. Republican hold.
# {{ushr|MA|6|X}}: [[John F. Tierney]] (D) lost renomination to [[Seth Moulton]] (D), who then won the general election. Democratic hold.
# {{ushr|Michigan|11|X}}: [[Kerry Bentivolio]] (R) lost renomination to [[Dave Trott (politician)|David Trott]] (R),<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.freep.com/article/20140805/NEWS06/308050217/Congress-11th-Bentivolio-Trott|title=Challenger Trott Defeats Bentivolio, Ends Tenure of 'Accidental Congressman'|newspaper=Detroit Free Press|access-date=August 10, 2014}}</ref> who then won the general election. Republican hold.
# {{ushr|TX|4|X}}: [[Ralph Hall]] (R), lost renomination to [[John Ratcliffe (American politician)|John Ratcliffe]] (R),<ref>{{cite news |url=http://trailblazersblog.dallasnews.com/2014/05/u-s-rep-ralph-hall-91-battles-john-ratcliffe-for-final-term.html |title=Oldest congressman, Ralph Hall, 91, ousted by John Ratcliffe |newspaper=The Dallas Morning News |access-date=May 27, 2014 |archive-date=May 29, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140529051448/http://trailblazersblog.dallasnews.com/2014/05/u-s-rep-ralph-hall-91-battles-john-ratcliffe-for-final-term.html/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> who then won the general election. Republican hold.
# {{ushr|VA|7|X}}: [[Eric Cantor]] (R) lost renomination to [[Dave Brat]] (R),<ref name="latimes06112014">{{cite news|last1=Barabak|first1=Mark Z.|title=The earthquake that toppled Eric Cantor: How did it happen?|url=http://www.latimes.com/nation/politics/politicsnow/la-pn-earthquake-toppled-cantor-20140611-story.html|newspaper=Los Angeles Times|access-date=June 11, 2014}}</ref> who then won the general election. Republican hold.

===In the general election===
Republicans had a net gain of nine seats, taken from Democrats.

==== Democrats ====
Twelve Democrats lost re-election to Republicans.
# {{ushr|Arizona|2|X}}: [[Ron Barber]] (D) lost to [[Martha McSally]] (R).
# {{ushr|Florida|26|X}}: [[Joe Garcia]] (D) lost to [[Carlos Curbelo]] (R).
# {{ushr|Georgia|12|X}}: [[John Barrow (U.S. politician)|John Barrow]] (D) lost to [[Rick W. Allen]] (R).
# {{ushr|Illinois|10|X}}: [[Brad Schneider]] (D) lost to [[Bob Dold]] (R).
# {{ushr|Illinois|12|X}}: [[William Enyart|Bill Enyart]] (D) lost to [[Mike Bost]] (R).
# {{ushr|Nevada|4|X}}: [[Steven Horsford]] (D) lost to [[Cresent Hardy]] (R).
# {{ushr|New Hampshire|1|X}}: [[Carol Shea-Porter]] (D) lost to [[Frank Guinta]] (R).
# {{ushr|NY|1|X}}: [[Tim Bishop]] (D) lost to [[Lee Zeldin]] (R).
# {{ushr|NY|24|X}}: [[Dan Maffei]] (D) lost to [[John Katko]] (R).
# {{ushr|TX|23|X}}: [[Pete Gallego]] (D) lost to [[Will Hurd]] (R).
# {{ushr|WV|3|X}}: [[Nick Rahall]] (D) lost to [[Evan Jenkins (politician)|Evan Jenkins]] (R).
# [[American Samoa's at-large congressional district|American Samoa]]: [[Eni Faleomavaega]] (D) lost to [[Amata Coleman Radewagen]] (R).

==== Republicans ====
Two Republicans lost re-election to Democrats.
# {{ushr|Florida|2|X}}: [[Steve Southerland (Florida politician)|Steve Southerland]] (R) lost to [[Gwen Graham]] (D).
# {{ushr|Nebraska|2|X}}: [[Lee Terry]] (R) lost to [[Brad Ashford]] (D).

==Open seat gains==
Republicans had a net gain of four seats previously held by Democrats.

=== Democratic to Republican ===
Five open seats previously held by Democrats were won by Republicans.
# {{ushr|Iowa|1|X}}: [[Bruce Braley]] (D) retired to run for U.S. Senate. Seat won by [[Rod Blum]] (R).
# {{ushr|Maine|2|X}}: [[Mike Michaud]] (D) retired to run for Governor of Maine. Seat won by [[Bruce Poliquin]] (R).
# {{ushr|NY|21|X}}: [[Bill Owens (New York politician)|Bill Owens]] (D) retired. Seat won by [[Elise Stefanik]] (R).
# {{ushr|NC|7|X}}: [[Mike McIntyre]] (D) retired. Seat won by [[David Rouzer]] (R).
# {{ushr|UT|4|X}}: [[Jim Matheson]] (D) retired. Seat won by [[Mia Love]] (R).

=== Republican to Democratic ===
One open seat previously held by a Republican was won by a Democrat.
# {{ushr|California|31|X}}: [[Gary Miller (politician)|Gary Miller]] (R) retired. Seat won by [[Pete Aguilar]] (D).

